{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/agents?scriptVersionId=367270488\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# AI Agents in LangChain\n\n## 1. What is an AI Agent?\n\nAn **AI Agent** is an intelligent system that receives a high-level goal from a user and autonomously plans, decides, and executes a sequence of actions using external tools, APIs, or knowledge sources.\n\nIt maintains context, reasons over multiple steps, adapts to new information, and works towards achieving the intended outcome.\n\nUnlike traditional applications, where users have to perform each step manually, AI agents can decide which actions need to be performed and in what order.\n\n## 2. Understanding AI Agents Through a Real-World Example\n\nLet's suppose we want to plan a budget-friendly trip from Delhi to Goa for 1st May to 7th May.\n\n### Traditional Approach (Manual Process)\n\nNormally, we would perform the following activities manually:\n\n1. **Book transportation:** Visit websites like MakeMyTrip or IRCTC to compare trains and flights, select travel dates, and book tickets.\n2. **Find accommodation:** Search for available hotels or hostels according to our budget and location preferences.\n3. **Plan local transportation:** Find and book cabs or rent scooters for travelling around Goa.\n4. **Explore attractions:** Research popular tourist attractions, beaches, restaurants, and activities.\n5. **Plan the itinerary:** Decide which places to visit on each day.\n6. **Calculate the budget:** Calculate transportation, accommodation, food, sightseeing, and other expenses.\n\nThis process involves considerable manual effort and human intervention.\n\n### AI Agent Approach\n\nNow imagine that MakeMyTrip integrates an AI Agent into its website, allowing users to interact with it through a chat interface.\n\nInstead of performing every activity manually, we simply provide our goal:\n\n**User Prompt:**\n\n\"Can you create a budget-friendly travel itinerary from Delhi to Goa from 1st May to 7th May?\"\n\nThe AI Agent understands the goal and independently plans the steps required to fulfil the request.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding User Intent\n\nThe agent first interprets the user's request and extracts the essential information.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Origin: Delhi\n- Destination: Goa\n- Travel dates: May 1–7\n- Preference: Budget travel\n- Goal: Plan a complete itinerary and optimize the overall cost.\n\nIt understands that the user needs more than just a travel ticket. They need transportation, accommodation, local movement, activities, and a complete travel plan.\n\n### Step 2: Researching Transportation Options\n\nThe agent uses external APIs or tools to research available transportation options.\n\nFor example:\n- IRCTC/Train API\n- Flight APIs\n\nIt compares:\n- Ticket prices\n- Travel duration\n- Availability\n\nAfter comparing the available options, it identifies a suitable option according to the user's budget.\n\nFor example, it suggests travelling by train from Delhi to Goa in 3AC.\n\n### Step 3: Searching for Affordable Accommodation\n\nNext, the agent searches for available hotels or hostels using hotel APIs or other search tools.\n\nIt applies filters based on the user's requirements:\n\n- Price: Less than ₹700 per night.\n- Location: Close to Baga or Anjuna Beach.\n- User reviews and ratings.\n\nFor example, it finds a dorm room at The Hosteller, Baga, for ₹650 per night.\n\nThe agent then asks the user for confirmation before proceeding with the booking.\n\n### Step 4: Planning Local Transportation\n\nOnce accommodation is sorted, the agent plans local transportation.\n\nFor example, it researches scooter rental options and suggests renting a scooter for approximately ₹300 per day.\n\nIt asks the user whether they would like to pre-book it.\n\n### Step 5: Creating the Travel Itinerary\n\nThe agent now combines the collected information and prepares a day-wise travel plan.\n\nFor example:\n\n- May 2: Baga, Calangute and Aguada Fort.\n- May 3: Old Goa churches, Fontainhas and local lunch.\n- May 4: South Goa day trip, including Palolem.\n- May 5: Anjuna flea market and sunset at Chapora.\n- May 6: Free day and beach café dinner.\n\nThe itinerary is created according to the user's preferences and available information.\n\n### Step 6: Planning the Return Journey\n\nThe agent also researches transportation options for the return journey on May 7.\n\nFor example:\n- Train: ₹800–₹11,500, overnight.\n- Early morning flight: Approximately ₹2,800.\n\nIt presents the available options and lets the user choose.\n\n### Step 7: Preparing the Final Budget\n\nFinally, the agent combines the estimated expenses into a budget summary.\n\nExample:\n\n| Expense | Estimated Cost |\n|---|---:|\n| Train (round trip) | ₹3,000 |\n| Stay (6 nights) | ₹3,900 |\n| Scooter + fuel | ₹2,200 |\n| Food | ₹2,500 |\n| Sightseeing & entry | ₹1,000 |\n| Shopping/miscellaneous | ₹1,500 |\n| **Total** | **₹14,100** |\n\nThe agent presents the complete budget and asks the user for confirmation.\n\n**Important:** These are illustrative figures from the example, not verified live travel prices.\n\n## 3. What Makes an AI Agent Different?\n\nThe main difference is that the user provides the goal rather than specifying every individual step.\n\nThe AI Agent decides:\n- What needs to be done.\n- Which tools are required.\n- In what sequence the tasks should be performed.\n- How to use the results of previous actions to make subsequent decisions.\n\nIt can also interact with the user when confirmation or additional information is required.\n\n## 4. Characteristics of AI Agents\n\n### 1. Goal-Driven\n\nYou tell the agent what you want to achieve, not exactly how to achieve it.\n\nFor example, instead of manually searching for tickets, hotels, and transportation, you simply ask it to plan a budget-friendly trip.\n\n### 2. Autonomous Planning\n\nThe agent can break down a complex problem into smaller tasks and decide the sequence in which those tasks should be performed.\n\nFor example, it divides travel planning into transportation, accommodation, local transportation, sightseeing, and budgeting.\n\n### 3. Tool-Using\n\nAI agents can interact with external tools, APIs, and knowledge sources to perform tasks and retrieve real-world information.\n\nFor example:\n- Train APIs for checking train availability.\n- Flight APIs for comparing flights.\n- Hotel APIs for finding accommodation.\n- Search tools for discovering tourist attractions.\n\n### 4. Context-Aware\n\nAn agent maintains relevant information from previous interactions and uses it while performing subsequent tasks.\n\nFor example, if the user has specified a budget-friendly trip, the agent considers that preference while searching for hotels, transportation, and activities.\n\n### 5. Adaptive\n\nAn agent can adapt its decisions when new information becomes available or when the user changes their requirements.\n\nFor example, if the user rejects a suggested hotel because it is too expensive, the agent can search for alternative accommodation within the user's budget.\n\n## 5. Key Takeaway\n\nAn AI Agent goes beyond simply generating text or answering questions. It understands a user's goal, plans the necessary steps, selects appropriate tools, executes actions, observes the results, and adapts its approach to accomplish the task.\n\n**In simple words:**\n\nAn LLM primarily generates responses, whereas an AI Agent uses an LLM's reasoning capabilities along with tools and actions to accomplish a goal.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n# ReAct, Agent and Agent Executor in LangChain\n\n## 1. ReAct (Reasoning + Acting)\n\nReAct is a design pattern used in AI agents. It stands for **Reasoning + Acting**.\n\nIt allows an LLM to interleave its reasoning (Thought) with external actions (such as tool usage) in a structured, multi-step process.\n\nInstead of generating an answer in one go, the model:\n1. Thinks about what needs to be done.\n2. Decides which action to take.\n3. Calls an appropriate tool if required.\n4. Observes the result returned by the tool.\n5. Uses the observation to decide the next step.\n6. Repeats the process until it can provide the final answer.\n\n### Why do we need ReAct?\n\nAn LLM may not always have the information or capabilities required to answer a question directly.\n\nFor example:\n- It may need current information from the internet.\n- It may need to perform calculations.\n- It may need to retrieve information from a database.\n\nReAct allows an LLM to use external tools while working through multi-step problems.\n\n### ReAct Example\n\n**User Query:**\n\n\"What is the capital of France and what is its population?\"\n\nThe agent can solve this in multiple steps:\n\n```text\nThought: I need to find the capital of France.\n\nAction: search_tool\n\nAction Input: \"capital of France\"\n\nObservation: Paris\n\nThought: Now I need to find the population of Paris.\n\nAction: search_tool\n\nAction Input: \"population of Paris\"\n\nObservation: 2.1 million\n\nThought: I now know the final answer.\n\nFinal Answer: Paris is the capital of France\nand has a population of approximately 2.1 million.\n```\n\nNotice that the agent does not try to solve everything in one step. It uses the result of the first action to decide what to do next.\n\n### Important Terms in ReAct\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Thought | The agent's reasoning about what to do next. |\n| Action | The tool or action selected by the agent. |\n| Action Input | The input or arguments provided to the tool. |\n| Observation | The result returned after executing the tool. |\n| Final Answer | The final response returned to the user. |\n\n**Important:** Thought → Action → Action Input → Observation can repeat multiple times until the agent generates a Final Answer.\n\n### Where is ReAct useful?\n\n- Multi-step problems.\n- Tool-augmented tasks.\n- Web searches and information retrieval.\n- Database lookups.\n- Tasks requiring multiple tool calls.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Agent and Agent Executor\n\nIn LangChain, an Agent and an Agent Executor have different responsibilities.\n\n### Agent\n\nAn Agent is responsible for deciding what should happen next.\n\nIt receives the user query and available context, then decides whether to:\n- Call a tool.\n- Select a particular tool.\n- Provide the required tool input.\n- Return a final answer.\n\nIn simple terms:\n\n**Agent = Decision Maker**\n\n### Agent Executor\n\nAgent Executor is responsible for orchestrating the entire execution loop.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\n1. Sends the user input and previous messages to the agent.\n2. Gets the next action from the agent.\n3. Executes the selected tool with the provided input.\n4. Collects the tool's observation.\n5. Adds the observation back into the conversation history.\n6. Calls the agent again with the updated history.\n7. Repeats until the agent returns a Final Answer.\n\nIn simple terms:\n\n**Agent Executor = Execution Manager**\n\n### Difference Between Agent and Agent Executor\n\n| Agent | Agent Executor |\n|---|---|\n| Decides what to do next. | Manages the complete execution loop. |\n| Selects the appropriate tool. | Executes the selected tool. |\n| Generates the next action. | Collects the tool observation. |\n| Determines when to provide the final answer. | Continues the loop until completion. |\n\n**Remember:**\n\nThe Agent decides, whereas the Agent Executor manages and executes the overall process.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ReAct Prompt Format\n\nFor an agent to follow the ReAct approach, it needs instructions describing the available tools and the expected output format.\n\nA ReAct prompt generally contains:\n\n1. Instructions about the task.\n2. Available tools and their descriptions.\n3. The expected reasoning and action format.\n4. The original user question.\n\n### Example Prompt\n\n```text\nAnswer the following questions as best you can.\nYou have access to the following tools:\n\nsearch_tool:\nUseful for answering general knowledge questions\nby querying a search API.\n\nUse the following format:\n\nQuestion: The input question you must answer\n\nThought: Think about what to do next.\n\nAction: The action to take. It should be one\nof the available tools.\n\nAction Input: The input to the action.\n\nObservation: The result of the action.\n\n... (This Thought/Action/Action Input/Observation\nsequence can repeat N times.)\n\nThought: I now know the final answer.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer to the original question.\n\nBegin!\n\nQuestion: What is the capital of France\nand what is its population?\n\nThought:\n```\n\nThe model follows this format to decide which tools to call and when to return the final answer.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Agent Executor Workflow (Complete Flow)\n\nThe complete workflow can be understood through the following steps:\n\n### Step 1: Receive User Query\n\nThe Agent Executor receives the original user query.\n\nExample:\n\n\"What is the capital of France and what is its population?\"\n\n### Step 2: Pass User Query + Agent Scratchpad to Agent\n\nThe Agent Executor sends:\n- The original user query.\n- The previous conversation history.\n- The Agent Scratchpad.\n\n**Agent Scratchpad:** It contains the intermediate steps generated during the agent's execution, such as previous thoughts, actions and observations.\n\nInitially, the scratchpad may be empty.\n\n### Step 3: Agent Response\n\nThe Agent processes the input and decides what to do next.\n\nThere are two possible outcomes:\n\n**Case 1: Agent Action**\n\nThe agent decides that it needs an external tool.\n\nIt returns an action containing:\n- Tool name.\n- Tool input.\n- Additional information about the action.\n\n**Case 2: Agent Finish**\n\nThe agent determines that it has enough information to answer the question.\n\nIt returns the final answer instead of requesting another tool call.\n\n### Step 4: Execute Tool\n\nIf the agent returns an Agent Action, the Agent Executor executes the selected tool using the provided input.\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nsearch_tool(\"capital of France\")\n```\n\n### Step 5: Collect Observation\n\nThe tool returns its result.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nObservation: Paris is the capital of France.\n```\n\nThe Agent Executor collects this observation.\n\n### Step 6: Update Scratchpad\n\nThe observation is added to the agent's intermediate execution history.\n\nThe updated scratchpad now contains the previous reasoning, action and observation.\n\n### Step 7: Loop Again\n\nThe Agent Executor sends the updated history back to the Agent.\n\nThe agent can now use the previous observation to decide its next action.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nThought: Now I need to find the population of Paris.\n\nAction: search_tool\n\nAction Input: \"population of Paris\"\n```\n\nThe Agent Executor executes this action and collects another observation.\n\nThis process repeats until the agent returns Agent Finish.\n\n### Step 8: Return Final Output\n\nWhen the agent returns Agent Finish, the Agent Executor stops the execution loop and returns the final output to the user.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Complete ReAct Execution Example\n\nLet's combine everything into one execution sequence.\n\n**User Query:**\n\n\"What is the capital of France and what is its population?\"\n\n| Execution | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | User submits the query. |\n| 2 | Agent Executor passes the query and scratchpad to the Agent. |\n| 3 | Agent decides to search for the capital of France. |\n| 4 | Agent returns an Agent Action. |\n| 5 | Agent Executor executes the search tool. |\n| 6 | Tool returns Paris as the observation. |\n| 7 | Agent Executor updates the scratchpad. |\n| 8 | Agent receives the updated history. |\n| 9 | Agent decides to search for the population of Paris. |\n| 10 | Agent Executor executes the second tool call. |\n| 11 | Tool returns the population as the observation. |\n| 12 | Agent Executor updates the scratchpad again. |\n| 13 | Agent determines that it has enough information and returns Agent Finish. |\n| 14 | Agent Executor returns the final answer to the user. |\n\n### Overall Workflow\n\n```text\nUser Query\n    |\n    v\nAgent Executor\n    |\n    v\nReceive User Query\n    |\n    v\nPass Query + Agent Scratchpad to Agent\n    |\n    v\nAgent Response\n    |\n    +-------------------------+\n    |                         |\n    v                         v\nAgent Action              Agent Finish\n    |                         |\n    v                         v\nExecute Tool              Return Final Output\n    |\n    v\nCollect Observation\n    |\n    v\nUpdate Scratchpad\n    |\n    |\n    +------ Loop back to Agent\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Takeaways\n\n1. ReAct stands for Reasoning + Acting.\n2. ReAct allows an LLM to combine reasoning with external tool usage.\n3. Thought, Action, Action Input and Observation are the main elements of the ReAct format.\n4. The Agent is responsible for deciding the next action.\n5. The Agent Executor manages the complete execution loop.\n6. The Agent Scratchpad maintains intermediate execution history.\n7. An Agent Action means the agent wants to execute a tool.\n8. Agent Finish means the agent has completed its task.\n9. The Agent Executor repeats the process until the agent returns a final answer.\n\n**Most important distinction to remember:**\n\n- ReAct = Reasoning and Acting design pattern.\n- Agent = Decides what action to take.\n- Agent Executor = Manages the execution loop.\n- Scratchpad = Stores intermediate steps and observations.\n","metadata":{}}]}